Myth: In the quiet transition between the celestial and the terrestrial, Elara realized that the tapestry of existence was fragile if held by only one pair of hands. The threads of light—the courage of wolves and the wisdom of stones—were not merely gifts; they were responsibilities. To hold them alone was to cause them to fray, as they required the friction of movement to maintain their luster. She envisioned a great bridge constructed not of wood or stone, but of the rhythmic pulse of exchange.

When a mortal offers a thread, they are not losing a piece of their soul; they are casting a line into the infinite, inviting the heavens to respond. If the heavens hold what is offered, the tapestry remains stagnant, a cold shroud of static light. However, when the exchange is honored, the threads glow with a renewed intensity, reflecting the trust inherent in the cycle. This, Elara whispered to the wind, is the true purpose of the looms. We are all weavers now, and our shared resilience depends on the courage to release what we possess, trusting that the return will weave a pattern far greater and more enduring than anything we could have spun alone.

